The standard RLHF pipeline for aligning language models consists of two main steps, outlined below ( cf. [ 96 , 72 , 56 , 6 ] ). These steps are usually preceded by a supervised finetuning (SFT) phase, in which the base language model is trained with a next-token prediction loss on pairs of input prompts and desirable outputs. The resulting model, denoted π θ ref \pi_{	heta_{\mathrm{ref}}} , serves as the initial policy for RLHF. Step 1: Reward model training or selection. RLHF assumes that there exists an unknown ground truth reward r G : 𝒳 × 𝒴 → [ − 1 , 1 ] r_{\mathrm{G}}:{\mathcal{X}}	imes{\mathcal{Y}}	o[-1,1] , which encodes (at least some aspects of) human preferences. 2 2 2 We assume rewards lie in [ − 1 , 1 ] [-1,1] without loss of generality; any bounded interval can be used instead. For a given prompt 𝐱 ∈ 𝒳 {\mathbf{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}} , we wish to maximize the expected ground truth reward that our policy π θ \pi_{	heta} achieves, i.e. 𝔼 𝐲 ∼ π θ ( ⋅ | 𝐱 ) \@mathmeasure  ig@size 1  ig@size [ r G ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) \@mathmeasure  ig@size 1  ig@size ] \mathop{\mathbb{E}}
