Section 3.2 showed that low reward variance hinders the efficiency of policy gradient. Notably, reward variance is independent of the measure typically used to evaluate reward models — accuracy [ 45 , 94 , 26 , 48 ] . Accuracy depends only on how the reward model ranks different outputs, disregarding the degree of separation between their rewards, whereas reward variance is determined by this degree of separation. A key implication, established by Theorem 2 , is that a reward model r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} can be accurate yet lead to arbitrarily slow ground truth reward maximization compared to a substantially less accurate model r RM ′ r^{\prime}_{\mathrm{RM}} , due to inducing low reward variance. For clarity, Theorem 2 considers: (i) the extreme case where r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} is perfectly accurate and r RM ′ r^{\prime}_{\mathrm{RM}} is almost completely inaccurate; and (ii) accuracy with respect to the uniform distribution over unordered output pairs, denoted by acc 𝐱 ⁡ ( r RM ) \acc_{\mathbf{x}}(r_{\mathrm{RM}}) for a prompt 𝐱 {\mathbf{x}} and reward model r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} . The detailed version of the theorem ( Theorem 5 in Appendix A ) is much more general, applying to almost any accuracy values for r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} and r RM ′ r^{\prime}_{\mathrm{RM}} , as measured with respect to any distribution over output pairs. Theorem 2 (Abridged version of Theorem 5 ) . Suppose π θ \pi_{	heta} is a tabular policy ( Equation 3 ). Given a prompt 𝐱 ∈ 𝒮 {\mathbf{x}}\in{\mathcal{S}} , let γ > 0 \gamma>0 and denote by t γ > 0 t_{\gamma}>0 the initial time at which 𝔼 𝐲 ∼ π θ ⁡ ( t ) ( ⋅ | 𝐱 ) \@mathmeasure  ig@size 1  ig@size [ r G ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) \@mathmeasure  ig@size 1  ig@size ] ≥ 𝔼 𝐲 ∼ π θ ⁡ ( 0 ) ( ⋅ | 𝐱 ) \@mathmeasure  ig@size 1  ig@size [ r G ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) \@mathmeasure  ig@size 1  ig@size ] + γ \mathop{\mathbb{E}}
