An alternative method to RLHF for aligning language models is Best-of-N sampling [ 53 ] . Instead of using a reward model r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} for policy gradient training, Best-of-N applies r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} only at test-time for choosing the best output from multiple candidates. Specifically, given a prompt 𝐱 ∈ 𝒳 {\mathbf{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}} , Best-of-N samples N ∈ ℕ N\in{\mathbb{N}} independent candidate outputs 𝐲 1 , … , 𝐲 N ∈ 𝒴 {\mathbf{y}}^{1},\ldots,{\mathbf{y}}^{N}\in{\mathcal{Y}} from the policy π \pi . Then, the output with highest reward according to r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} is returned. Theorem 2 ( Section 3.3 ) showed that perfectly accurate reward models ( i.e. , reward models that rank all outputs correctly) can underperform less accurate models in RLHF. In contrast, for Best-of-N, Proposition 1 establishes that perfectly accurate reward models are always optimal. This highlights that the role of accuracy — and, more broadly, what makes a good reward model — can vary depending on the choice of alignment method. Proposition 1 . For any policy π \pi and reward model r RM : 𝒳 × 𝒴 → [ − 1 , 1 ] r_{\mathrm{RM}}:{\mathcal{X}}	imes{\mathcal{Y}}	o[-1,1] , let π r RM BoN \pi^{\mathrm{BoN}}_{r_{\mathrm{RM}}} be the Best-of-N policy induced by π \pi and r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} , for N ∈ ℕ N\in{\mathbb{N}} . Given a prompt 𝐱 ∈ 𝒳 {\mathbf{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}} , suppose that r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} has perfect accuracy, i.e., acc 𝐱 , 𝒟 ⁡ ( r RM ) = 1 \acc_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathcal{D}}}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({r_{\mathrm{RM}}}ight)}}=1 for any distribution 𝒟 {\mathcal{D}} over unordered output pairs. Then, r RM r_{\mathrm{RM}} is optimal for Best-of-N in the sense that: 𝔼 𝐲 ∼ π BoN r RM ( ⋅ | 𝐱 ) [ r G ​ ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) ] = max r RM ′ : 𝒳 × 𝒴 → [ − 1 , 1 ] 𝔼 𝐲 ∼ π BoN r RM ′ ( ⋅ | 𝐱 ) [ r G ​ ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) ] . \mathop{\mathbb{E}}
